<feed xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><title>Breaking news from AFRICA, Maghreb and Sub-Saharan Africa - France 24</title><subtitle type="html">FRANCE 24’s coverage of events, politics and social issues across Africa, including North Africa (the Maghreb) and the Sahel, aided by our network of correspondents and experts across the continent.</subtitle><id>https://www.france24.com/en/africa/rss</id><updated>2026-09-29T14:56:16Z</updated><author><name>rssync</name></author><link rel="self" href="rss" type="application/atom+xml" /><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/africa/" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/africa/rss" type="application/rss+xml" /><entry><title>Ebola a 'terrifying disease': Conflict-ravaged DRC faces overburdened healthcare system</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:828cb19e983997f77f7411514804a4d27a73b20fe7d1f3f419601ce765302414</id><updated>2026-09-29T14:56:16Z</updated><published>2026-09-29T10:09:02Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/video/20260929-ebola-a-terrifying-disease-as-drc-faces-decades-of-conflict-and-overburdened-healthcare-sys--656d2bdf0d4d.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20260929-ebola-a-terrifying-disease-as-drc-faces-decades-of-conflict-and-overburdened-healthcare-system-1" /><summary type="html">Eve Irvine is pleased to welcome Stephanie Hoffmann, Project Coordinator for Butembo, North Kivu, Doctors Without Borders. The Ebola outbreak is hitting a region already ravaged by decades of armed conflict, insecurity, and an overwhelmed health system. As the epidemic's epicenter shifts from Ituri to North Kivu, the disease is spreading and the transmission is accelerating. In Butembo, a densely populated city of around 2 million, health workers themselves are being placed in an impossible position, often caring for their neighbors and relatives without adequate protective equipment.</summary></entry><entry><title>Ethiopian forces claim to have retaken key Tigray town</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:dc82ab0fd1cdf5dc0ccfdd35129e037d951fb7d52976c4a0305e760a06573c65</id><updated>2026-09-29T14:54:10Z</updated><published>2026-09-28T20:32:38Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/ethiopian-forces-claim-tigray-town--a7d829132c01.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/ethiopian-forces-claim-tigray-town" /><summary type="html">In tonight's edition: Ethiopia's army says it's seized a key city in the northern region of Tigray amidst escalating conflict with local rebels. Also, two mass shootings in South Africa leave at least 28 dead. Plus Ugandan activist Praise Aloikin Opoloje is facing court after she turned up following days of uncertainty about her whereabouts.</summary><category term="Eye on Africa" /></entry><entry><title>Trump granting asylum to Afrikaners is 'race-based policy', South African FM claims</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:c62eadeb638ea773a9c73f4ca893b9f1eee83708b0ef652f5207c771545028b8</id><updated>2026-09-29T14:54:10Z</updated><published>2026-09-28T10:38:43Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/t_te-_-t_te/20260928-trump-granting-asylum-to-afrikaners-is-race-based-policy-south-african-fm-claims--ce5df4a22d5f.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/t%C3%AAte-%C3%A0-t%C3%AAte/20260928-trump-granting-asylum-to-afrikaners-is-race-based-policy-south-african-fm-claims" /><summary type="html">In an interview with FRANCE 24 on the sidelines of the UN General Assembly in New York, South African Foreign Minister Ronald Lamola said Washington's plan to take in up to 17,500 more White South Africans was "a race-based policy", dismissing the "so-called genocide" of Afrikaners as "fake news".</summary><category term="Tête à tête" /></entry><entry><title>Ethiopia accuses Eritrea, Sudan of backing Tigray rebels amid clashes</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:637c00a9f0aa60ae3bb32dbb9a8cfb275ca0c7c679fa5b4111f4d7d3151ba85a</id><updated>2026-09-29T14:54:10Z</updated><published>2026-09-27T23:27:29Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/africa/20260927-ethiopia-accuses-eritrea-sudan-backing-tigray-rebels--b54da5cdf6a1.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/africa/20260927-ethiopia-accuses-eritrea-sudan-backing-tigray-rebels" /><summary type="html">Ethiopia’s army chief accused Eritrea and Sudan on Sunday of backing armed groups fighting federal troops, as clashes with Tigrayan rebels intensified. The Tigray People’s Liberation Front said it was waging a “defensive war” and seized airports. A humanitarian source said rebels captured the Afar town of Erebti on Sunday.</summary><category term="Africa" /></entry><entry><title>South Africa shootings leave 27 killed</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:c70f2e21cffc0e1645585746e1869aa98c96f3db4f3c04c81aba5e1535559253</id><updated>2026-09-29T14:54:10Z</updated><published>2026-09-27T13:56:20Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/south-africa-shootings-leave-27-killed--c505b1841bd3.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/south-africa-shootings-leave-27-killed" /><summary type="html">It has been a deadly night in South Africa, with 27 people killed in two separate shooting incidents. Police say 17 people were shot dead at a tavern outside Johannesburg by suspects armed with assault rifles, while another 10 were killed near Cape Town when gunmen opened fire at a venue selling grilled meat. No arrests have been made as police search for the attackers. Initial investigations suggest one shooting may have been linked to a turf war between illegal miners, while the other could have stemmed from a business rivalry. France 24 correspondent Eurice Masson reports the latest from South Africa.</summary></entry><entry><title>Femicides on the rise in South Africa</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:c9e8fec906cee417deb654b3a2531d29c3e56556592834ac2190eb025d0c2008</id><updated>2026-09-29T14:54:10Z</updated><published>2026-09-27T13:51:12Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/femicides-on-the-rise-in-south-africa--439af0aa2c75.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/femicides-on-the-rise-in-south-africa" /><summary type="html">Two more women have been found dead in Johannesburg this weekend, bringing the number of women killed in a single municipality to 11 in just two months. Most of the other victims were in their 20s and 30s, with some found partially undressed and showing signs of sexual assault, raising fears that a serial killer could be responsible. Police have offered a $25,000 reward for information leading to the arrest of the suspected killer or killers. The deaths have sparked a national outcry over violence against women in South Africa, which has one of the world’s highest rates of gender-based violence. Tegan Snayman, Research, Campaigns and Communications Assistant at Amnesty International South Africa, shares further insights.</summary></entry><entry><title>Gunmen kill at least 27 people in separate mass shootings in South Africa</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:d5517ff1bb9905b9d499e568b2ad897f194a0e7ac08b2987967c958a20bc9bcc</id><updated>2026-09-29T14:54:10Z</updated><published>2026-09-27T12:34:08Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/africa/20260927-gunmen-kill-27-people-in-separate-mass-shootings-in-south-africa--48f7dc4b3928.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/africa/20260927-gunmen-kill-27-people-in-separate-mass-shootings-in-south-africa" /><summary type="html">At least 27 people were killed in separate mass shootings in Cape Town and Johannesburg, police said Sunday. No suspects have been arrested in either shooting, and the motives for the attacks remain unknown.</summary><category term="Africa" /></entry><entry><title>‘Super El Niño’ deepens hunger crisis in South Sudan says UN</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:06a216a608ebae244d90a4d5f6eb6fbbb71a2a35299ded83683a678c2a5a0ac6</id><updated>2026-09-29T14:54:10Z</updated><published>2026-09-26T10:06:43Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/video/20260926-super-el-ni_o-deepens-hunger-crisis-in-south-sudan-says-un--c820ea8f62e0.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20260926-super-el-ni%C3%B1o-deepens-hunger-crisis-in-south-sudan-says-un" /><summary type="html">"The already dire hunger crisis in South Sudan is becoming increasingly critical as violence, extreme weather and dwindling aid leave millions without enough food" says UN.</summary></entry><entry><title>Morocco’s Authenticity and Modernity Party wins parliamentary election</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:4a3fba345af0e6e1936d2ee15d5e6f3b1cbbd167d952cd0311ea32a3449e3045</id><updated>2026-09-29T14:54:10Z</updated><published>2026-09-26T09:38:30Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/morocco-s-authenticity-and-modernity-party-wins-parliamentary-election--1daba84b0262.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/morocco-s-authenticity-and-modernity-party-wins-parliamentary-election" /><summary type="html">Morocco’s Authenticity and Modernity Party won the most seats in the country’s parliamentary election, taking 97 of 395 seats in the House of Representatives, according to final results announced by the Interior Ministry on Friday. The party, founded by a royal adviser close to King Mohammed VI and known by its French acronym PAM, fell short of an absolute majority and will need to negotiate with other parties to form a governing coalition.</summary></entry><entry><title>Heavy fighting on multiple fronts between Ethiopian forces and rebel groups</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:6ca3c6fd3a7bb20920fa6dfcb2a54ef7e8657a1dd8cb779ebdf8782a204409a7</id><updated>2026-09-29T14:54:10Z</updated><published>2026-09-26T09:26:41Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/heavy-fighting-on-multiple-fronts-between-ethiopian-forces-and-rebel-groups--e78db56fc270.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/heavy-fighting-on-multiple-fronts-between-ethiopian-forces-and-rebel-groups" /><summary type="html">Fighting appears to be worsening in Ethiopia after the TPLF said it was in a "full blown" blown war with federal forces this week, days after the Tigrayan rebels declared a coalition with six other armed groups across the country, all with the objective of overthrowing prime minister abiy ahmed's government. And as of friday, it appears that another armed group has claimed to have joined the alliance. More from our correspondent in Addis Ababa Tom Canetti.</summary></entry><entry><title>Telecoms disruption raises fears as heavy fighting strikes Ethiopia</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:04f334d351da28057a469ae0b35823f9495197350c400413e4570b0711a0b195</id><updated>2026-09-29T14:54:10Z</updated><published>2026-09-25T20:32:28Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/eye-on-africa/20260925-telecoms-disruption-raises-fears-as-heavy-fighting-strikes-ethiopia--7d5d81c48ccb.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/eye-on-africa/20260925-telecoms-disruption-raises-fears-as-heavy-fighting-strikes-ethiopia" /><summary type="html">First, fighting continues between Ethiopian forces and the Tigray People's Liberation Front as fears of a regional spillover continue to mount. Next, in DR Congo, both the opposition and the ruling UDPS party continue to blame each other for the violence at a mass protest 10 days ago. Plus the Authenticity and Modernity Party looks to form a coalition in Morocco to address top voter issues, including the cost-of-living crisis.</summary><category term="Eye on Africa" /></entry><entry><title>Each side feels it 'could get a decisive military victory' in Sudan, Trump's Africa envoy says</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:62f98e5d02a43ad69b7f104fe21a60f205f56e3e4362b86e7a938ac05da2f055</id><updated>2026-09-29T14:54:10Z</updated><published>2026-09-25T12:54:12Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/t_te-_-t_te/20260925-each-side-feels-it-could-get-a-decisive-military-victory-in-sudan-trump-s-africa-envoy-says--9207dc16119d.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/t%C3%AAte-%C3%A0-t%C3%AAte/20260925-each-side-feels-it-could-get-a-decisive-military-victory-in-sudan-trump-s-africa-envoy-says" /><summary type="html">In an interview with FRANCE 24 on the sidelines of the UN General Assembly, Trump's Africa envoy Massad Boulos said that each side in Sudan feels it "could get a decisive military victory". Asked about Western Sahara, he acknowledged there is no "imminent solution that we can feel or touch". He also rejected Rwanda's accusations of bias over US sanctions, saying they "can easily be reversed".</summary><category term="Tête à tête" /></entry><entry><title>Ethiopia: what's driving the renewed violence in Tigray?</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:243ae525985c414c6342002d05f00706e5a3e9249bd2c5d76d7ea6539bb2eed3</id><updated>2026-09-29T14:54:10Z</updated><published>2026-09-25T11:15:52Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/ethiopia-what-s-driving-the-renewed-violence-in-tigray--2c6abb0159c4.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/ethiopia-what-s-driving-the-renewed-violence-in-tigray" /><summary type="html">Tigray's rebel authorities say they are now in a "full-blown war" with the Ethiopian government, as fighting spreads into the neighbouring Amhara and Afar regions. Ethiopia's federal army says 272 rebels were killed and 260 wounded in the north, while FRANCE 24 spoke to Jervin Naidoo, Political Analyst at Oxford Economics Africa, to assess the seriousness of the escalation and explain what is driving the renewed violence in northern Ethiopia</summary></entry><entry><title>Senegal: power cuts return as Dakar swelters in the heat</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:d41fb577f5bbdac4d70369d93677b6ce73ba42734e6df0c93c7be12f653e0d0d</id><updated>2026-09-29T14:54:10Z</updated><published>2026-09-25T11:14:40Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/senegal-power-cuts-return-as-dakar-swelters-in-the-heat--fd18df6442db.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/senegal-power-cuts-return-as-dakar-swelters-in-the-heat" /><summary type="html">After years of largely stabilising its electricity supply, Senegal is once again facing power cuts, with outages returning to Dakar in September. The blackouts are fuelling frustration among residents as the capital endures a spell of intense heat.</summary></entry><entry><title>Ethiopia: Tigray rebels claim "full-blown war"</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:f24cb2520013bf1afd15dce94dd1a6bdecdb8a1d2659f74b8b9ab82a474e4fe8</id><updated>2026-09-29T14:54:10Z</updated><published>2026-09-25T11:13:48Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/ethiopia-tigray-rebels-claim-full-blown-war--9b074c24592a.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/ethiopia-tigray-rebels-claim-full-blown-war" /><summary type="html">The Tigray People's Liberation Front says it is in a "full-blown war" with Ethiopia's federal government after seizing key airports and launching attacks into the neighbouring Amhara and Afar regions. The escalation comes days after the TPLF joined six other armed groups in a new alliance against the government, raising fears of a return to the devastating 2020-2022 conflict that killed at least 600,000 people.</summary></entry><entry><title>Ethiopia: renewed Tigray fighting raises fears of return to civil war</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:3ee388ce9bca3ea082cdab2e4acf254a8ec33e87083e7aa690a8cb61eb70e55f</id><updated>2026-09-29T14:54:10Z</updated><published>2026-09-25T11:12:34Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/ethiopia-renewed-tigray-fighting-raises-fears-of-return-to-civil-war--a8794692057f.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/ethiopia-renewed-tigray-fighting-raises-fears-of-return-to-civil-war" /><summary type="html">Fears are mounting that Ethiopia could be sliding back towards civil war as fighting intensifies in the north, with the army saying it killed 272 TPLF fighters in Amhara while the rebels describe the conflict as a "full-blown war." The escalation comes after the TPLF joined a new alliance with six other armed groups. FRANCE 24's Tom Canetti has the latest from the ground.</summary></entry><entry><title>Tigray rebels in 'full-blown' war with government</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:08db7e225ef1c97582b09808d58817432e19b95abfaf99593b138edc2e881de8</id><updated>2026-09-29T14:54:10Z</updated><published>2026-09-24T20:23:08Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/tigray-rebels-in-full-blown-war-with-government--26a3e13754ca.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/tigray-rebels-in-full-blown-war-with-government" /><summary type="html">In tonight's edition: Tigrayans in northern Ethiopia start panic-buying supplies in the rush to stock up amid fears that months of tensions have finally tipped over into full-blown conflict. Also, power cuts return to Senegal. Plus Yemi Alade, the Nigerian superstar, is out with a new album and speaks to FRANCE 24.</summary><category term="Eye on Africa" /></entry><entry><title>A look back at Tunisia's summer of shortages</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:79211f7b21b577c02369265c37fc9dc6e8a1eb2ee714967676a1897ecb0e5086</id><updated>2026-09-29T14:54:10Z</updated><published>2026-09-24T09:43:31Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/the-observers/20260924-look-back-tunisiassummer-shortages--1433d0807877.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/the-observers/20260924-look-back-tunisiassummer-shortages" /><summary type="html">Repeated power and water outages, long queues for bottled water, and poultry farms destroyed by the heat: millions of Tunisians were affected by water and electricity shortages over the summer. Our Observers say this is the result of years of underinvestment. The water shortage eased in late August when authorities sent millions of bottles of water to stores, but many are concerned the same thing could happen next summer.</summary><category term="The Observers" /></entry><entry><title>Tigray rebels seize airports</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:f7387177667b216a31fa8fbb3eb9824efcbd8f0ebe643d880f8039e9a04cfc90</id><updated>2026-09-29T14:54:10Z</updated><published>2026-09-23T20:27:14Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/tigray-rebels-seize-airports--6203b2cdd57b.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/tigray-rebels-seize-airports" /><summary type="html">In tonight's edition: TPLF fighters take over the airport in Tigray amid escalating conflict with federal troops. But first, concerns about South Africa's high rates of crimes are brought even more into focus as 11 people are killed at a party. And in eastern DR Congo, residents of Bukavu are once again searching through the ruins of their homes after another deadly fire.</summary><category term="Eye on Africa" /></entry><entry><title>Morocco’s centre-right PAM leads in provisional election results</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:c4c48b2c6f0d5f8f2a5cd1447b96809f9a3b06055d812c3d9d4c4a23293fb850</id><updated>2026-09-29T14:54:10Z</updated><published>2026-09-23T13:57:36Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/africa/20260923-morocco-s-centre-right-pam-secures-most-seats-in-election-provisional-results--18ecdd8f84ad.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/africa/20260923-morocco-s-centre-right-pam-secures-most-seats-in-election-provisional-results" /><summary type="html">Morocco’s Authenticity and Modernity Party (PAM) won the largest number of seats in the country’s parliamentary elections, according to provisional results announced by the Interior Ministry Thursday. PAM, which is part of the kingdom's ruling coalition, made an effort to woo young voters by recruiting the head of the Royal Moroccan Football Federation on the campaign trail.</summary><category term="Africa" /></entry><entry><title>Fresh drilling for a lost Egyptian queen?</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:a70e20b22ed237b9688a2b0a794578d64b18dd1329d7ddc5c474ce18912b31b9</id><updated>2026-09-29T14:54:10Z</updated><published>2026-09-23T10:31:13Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/press-review/20260923-fresh-drilling-for-a-lost-egyptian-queen--96525c3c0aab.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/press-review/20260923-fresh-drilling-for-a-lost-egyptian-queen" /><summary type="html">PRESS REVIEW – Wednesday, September 23: The papers look ahead to US President Donald Trump hosting his Chinese counterpart Xi Jinping. In other news, a Haitian-born novelist, tipped to win France's prestigious Goncourt literary prize, has been struck by an AI scandal. Finally, could the remains of Nefertiti lie behind the burial chamber of Tutankhamun?</summary><category term="Press Review" /></entry><entry><title>ICC convicts Central African Republic rebel leader of crimes against humanity</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:a8ddf0c4c65615034e659bc13c86a1246304846a68be6d087f5758f78f2482df</id><updated>2026-09-29T14:54:10Z</updated><published>2026-09-23T10:27:34Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/africa/20260923-icc-convicts-central-african-republic-rebel-leader-of-crimes-against-humanity--d6e9d0e23f7a.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/africa/20260923-icc-convicts-central-african-republic-rebel-leader-of-crimes-against-humanity" /><summary type="html">The International Criminal Court on Wednesday convicted former Central African Republic militia commander Mahamat Said Abdel Kani of torture and unlawful imprisonment. Said oversaw a detention centre in the capital Bangui infamous for its brutal conditions during the country's sectarian civil war.</summary><category term="Africa" /></entry><entry><title>Moroccans vote in parliamentary elections after migrant rush to Ceuta</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:4c8163a19f30adc8421a56fd461ce045208b92c370a97f6da7cf9629a6a8731e</id><updated>2026-09-29T14:54:10Z</updated><published>2026-09-23T07:14:59Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/africa/20260923-moroccans-vote-in-parliamentary-elections-after-migrant-rush-to-ceuta--6d3270907457.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/africa/20260923-moroccans-vote-in-parliamentary-elections-after-migrant-rush-to-ceuta" /><summary type="html">Moroccan voters choose new parliament Wednesday in elections dominated by economic concerns after a mass migration surge to the Spanish enclave of Ceuta in July highlighted rising youth unemployment and cost of living concerns. Nearly 16 million Moroccans have registered to elect 395 members of the House of Representatives.</summary><category term="Africa" /></entry><entry><title>‘I had never heard of Eswatini’: Deported by the US to a country that rejects them</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:0d3792f0ca4188695504728f7968282d881309a35e74ff1809924412e49aca6e</id><updated>2026-09-29T14:54:10Z</updated><published>2026-09-23T06:56:42Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/africa/20260923-never-heard-eswatini-deported-by-us-country-that-rejects-them-forbidden-stories--9a79002fea05.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/africa/20260923-never-heard-eswatini-deported-by-us-country-that-rejects-them-forbidden-stories" /><summary type="html">Migrants previously convicted by US courts have been imprisoned in Africa’s last absolute monarchy, Eswatini, over the past several months. Since being deported from the US, they have found themselves in a country about which they know nothing, and where local residents look on these “criminal” arrivals with suspicion. Their abrupt journey to the small southern African kingdom stems from a secret deal struck by Trump administration as part of his programme of mass deportations investigated by “The Deportation Project”, undertaken by Forbidden Stories and a network of 72 journalists across 15 countries.</summary><category term="Africa" /></entry><entry><title>US refusing to give Sudan's de facto leader visa for UN</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:2a27d5b3da39cb6e81940d690826ab5b35a8af386936c4a1e1e46add5ff9cc04</id><updated>2026-09-29T14:54:10Z</updated><published>2026-09-22T20:13:30Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/us-refusing-to-give-sudan-s-de-facto-leader-visa-for-un--35682f8a2d46.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/us-refusing-to-give-sudan-s-de-facto-leader-visa-for-un" /><summary type="html">In tonight's edition: The United States is withholding a visa for Sudanese army chief Abdel Fattah al-Burhan ahead of his scheduled UN appearance. Also, Ghana is beefing up its drugs authority in the wake of major drug busts in France, Belgium and South Africa. Plus it’s back-to-school season in Ivory Coast.</summary><category term="Eye on Africa" /></entry><entry><title>Lake Chad, a region under threat from climate change</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:7a970c9aea956e93184989ea884ddd30f8e1f226552ad00ac844ef3b70061d08</id><updated>2026-09-29T14:54:10Z</updated><published>2026-09-22T11:30:56Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/focus/20260922-climate-change-overfishing-lake-chad-sees-fish-stocks-dwindle--8bb3fa3f65e0.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/focus/20260922-climate-change-overfishing-lake-chad-sees-fish-stocks-dwindle" /><summary type="html">People living in Chad’s Lake Province are bearing the brunt of climate change. The region and the communities that have developed there have historically depended on this vast body of water. Fishing, first and foremost, but also farming, are the pillars of local livelihoods. Yet both are increasingly disrupted by erratic weather patterns: dwindling fish stocks, damaged farmland and a rising cost of living.</summary><category term="Focus" /></entry><entry><title>Ethiopia's main armed groups form anti-government alliance</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:9e846271fd900d708dfb66a96210899122e1cef17e6385611f0e7fbfd73bc658</id><updated>2026-09-29T14:54:10Z</updated><published>2026-09-21T21:16:13Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/ethiopia-s-main-armed-groups-form-anti-government-alliance--d7f1443afa0c.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/ethiopia-s-main-armed-groups-form-anti-government-alliance" /><summary type="html">In tonight's edition: A coalition of opposition groups band together in the hope of ousting Ethiopian Prime Minister Abiy Ahmed. Also, we speak to the deputy chief of the UN's peacekeeping mission in DR Congo. And with just a few days to go before legislative elections, young Moroccans are torn between hope and scepticism ahead of the crucial vote.</summary><category term="Eye on Africa" /></entry><entry><title>Deportation project: 72 journalists investigate US treatment of migrants</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:efd92ac7015e413c3c07b618a1f123a81e39fdfa8279e7c961dce3a07cea359d</id><updated>2026-09-29T14:54:10Z</updated><published>2026-09-21T08:55:07Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/video/20260921-deportation-project-72-journalists-investigate-us-treatment-of-migrants-1--dea57e7ef702.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20260921-deportation-project-72-journalists-investigate-us-treatment-of-migrants-1" /><summary type="html">Under secretive deals with countries across Africa and Latin America, the Trump administration has deported thousands of migrants to countries they had never previously visited. Of the 25,000 deportations documented by reporters, the vast majority were sent across the border to Mexico. One of the journalists who investigated the story, Sofía Álvarez Jurado, explains how these people are forced across the borders to Mexico and what happens to them once they arrive.</summary></entry><entry><title>Investigation: In Cameroon, the victims of Trump’s deportations face uncertain fates</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:8cf4471f7146d4e6929ac8708d5c9442cfb7a1b5f22067b4f97f87e17f7fe011</id><updated>2026-09-29T14:54:10Z</updated><published>2026-09-21T05:00:25Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/africa/20260921-waiting-for-us-to-crack-victims-of-trump-deportations-face-uncertain-fates-in-cameroon--49f56fccb149.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/africa/20260921-waiting-for-us-to-crack-victims-of-trump-deportations-face-uncertain-fates-in-cameroon" /><summary type="html">Under often-secret deals struck with third countries across Africa and Latin America, US President Donald Trump has deported thousands of people who were hoping to build new lives in the US to countries they’ve never set foot in before. Working with Forbidden Stories, FRANCE 24 investigated the stories of several African migrants whose lives were thrown into chaos by their abrupt deportation from the US to a temporary shelter in Cameroon’s capital Yaoundé.</summary><category term="Africa" /></entry><entry><title>Deported to Africa: An international investigation into Trump’s migrant policy</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:91810cbb3b4675443da8f41956a919e2f309a09b2e6f9624384174e18b5fa8f5</id><updated>2026-09-29T14:54:10Z</updated><published>2026-09-21T05:00:20Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/africa/20260921-deported-to-africa-an-international-investigation-into-trump-s-migrant-policy--a08a651759dd.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/africa/20260921-deported-to-africa-an-international-investigation-into-trump-s-migrant-policy" /><summary type="html">As part of a consortium of 26 media outlets coordinated by Forbidden Stories, FRANCE 24 and RFI spent seven months investigating the secret deals struck between the administration of US President Donald Trump and several African countries. These “third countries” have agreed to host undocumented migrants detained by ICE and deported from the United States.</summary><category term="Africa" /></entry></feed>
